{
  "title": "Sylius \u2014 ecommerce (8,473\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Sylius\nDomain: ecommerce | Scale: large | Stars: 8,473\nProblem: Sylius provides an open-source, headless eCommerce framework built on PHP/Symfony that enables developers to build highly customized online stores with a powerful REST API. It addresses the need for a flexible, developer-friendly eCommerce solution that avoids the rigidity of traditional monolithic shop platforms.\nTarget users: eCommerce developers, PHP/Symfony developers, Digital agencies building custom online stores, Businesses requiring tailored eCommerce solutions, B2B eCommerce operators\nCore features: Headless eCommerce with REST API (API Platform); Product catalog and inventory management; Shopping cart and checkout process; Order management and fulfillment; Admin panel for store management; Plugin/extension system via Sylius Store; Multi-store and multi-channel support (Plus); Loyalty system and returns handling (Plus)\nIntegrations: API Platform, Symfony UX Autocomplete, Symfony UX Live Component, Hotwire Stimulus, Packagist (sylius-plugin ecosystem)\nTech stack: Twig, SCSS, JavaScript, Symfony UX / Stimulus, Tom Select + PHP, Symfony Framework + Doctrine ORM (implied by Symfony/Sylius stack) + Docker, Makefile, Shell scripts\nKey libraries: API Platform, PHPUnit, Behat, Symfony UX Live Component, Symfony UX Autocomplete, @hotwired/stimulus, tom-select, Webpack Encore\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- BDD-first development culture: the large Gherkin test suite (1.5MB) indicates full-stack Behavior-Driven Development is a first-class concern, not an afterthought.\n- Symfony Bundle modularization: the codebase is split into discrete Symfony Bundles (AdminBundle, ShopBundle, etc.), enabling consumers to use only the components they need.\n- Headless-first API design using API Platform allows the same backend to power diverse frontends (web, mobile, third-party integrations) without coupling to a single rendering layer.\n- Commercial Plus tier layered on open-source core: advanced features (multi-store, B2B, loyalty) are offered as paid modules, demonstrating an open-core business model pattern.",
